## 40. Final Research Contract

The Vetted SaaS Blueprint research system must behave like a **compounding commercial intelligence engine**, not an endless scraper.

It succeeds when:

- research begins from a defined decision or intelligence need;
- existing knowledge is checked before new acquisition;
- validated prior research remains useful when no new information exists;
- daily research strengthens or confirms the knowledge base;
- recent conversations reveal what people care about;
- authoritative sources establish product facts;
- Scrapling provides a strong free-first acquisition path without becoming an architectural prison;
- last30days provides a strong recent-intelligence path without being mistaken for universal evidence;
- provider details can change without rewriting the editorial system;
- customer language improves relevance, SEO, AEO, and conversion communication;
- uncertainty is preserved;
- changed facts trigger targeted refresh;
- no-change research is accepted;
- page workers receive prepared intelligence;
- simpler models are given a fair opportunity to execute bounded work efficiently;
- premium reasoning remains an escalation path;
- unfinished automation never becomes an excuse for publishing nothing;
- every active day leaves Vetted SaaS Blueprint with more public value, more reusable intelligence, or preferably both.

> **Research continuously. Reuse aggressively. Detect change precisely. Preserve uncertainty. Build from prepared intelligence. Ship every day.**

## 1. Purpose

This document defines how Vetted SaaS Blueprint turns its daily connected three-page decision set into sustained discovery, trusted distribution, repeat visits, qualified commercial action, and long-term audience value.

It governs:

- demand sensing;
- publication-led distribution;
- organic search and Answer Engine Optimization;
- AI crawler access, monitoring, control, and future monetisation;
- internal discovery and topic relationships;
- owned-audience development;
- email and evidence-change alerts;
- earned authority, citations, and partnerships;
- community and visual distribution experiments;
- machine-readable feeds, embeds, and approved reuse;
- post-publication refresh and redistribution;
- growth measurement and experiment governance;
- ethical affiliate conversion;
- distribution resilience when a channel or dependency fails.

The strategy exists to attract real people who need help making software decisions. It is not a plan for manufacturing traffic, impressions, or content volume without user value.

## 2. Authority and boundaries

This document is authoritative for growth, distribution, audience development, and channel measurement.

**Cadence supersession (owner directive 2026-10-28):** Any "daily", "every active day", fixed-time, scheduled, watchdog, rescue, or recurring-publication wording in this document is retained only as historical batch terminology or traceability. It does not authorise a schedule or ongoing publication obligation. The release unit remains valid, but execution occurs only from an explicit manual or API trigger under `AGENTS.md` and `planning.md`.

It does not redefine:

- the product promise or functional requirements in the Master PRD;
- the three public page types in the Product Roadmap and Master PRD;
- evidence acquisition rules in the Research Operations document;
- editorial assembly and refresh rules in the Editorial Operations document;
- design and interaction rules in the Design System;
- deployment mechanics in the Architecture and Implementation Standards documents.

Where a distribution tactic conflicts with truthfulness, editorial independence, user usefulness, privacy, accessibility, or the daily delivery obligation, those requirements take priority.

The words **MUST**, **SHOULD**, and **MAY** are deliberate:

- **MUST** means mandatory.
- **SHOULD** means expected unless a documented reason supports a safer or more useful alternative.
- **MAY** means optional, experimental, or dependent on evidence of value.

### 3.1 Daily connected release

Every active operating day MUST publish and deploy one connected decision set:

1. one **Decision Hub**;
2. one **Solution Hub**;
3. one **Decision Scenario**.

The three pages MUST address the same customer problem, outcome, category, or buying situation. They are one distribution unit, not three unrelated articles.

### 3.2 Delivery never waits for evidence completeness

Evidence availability MUST NOT stop publication or deployment.

The set publishes with its actual evidence state:

- confirmed;
- partially confirmed;
- conflicting;
- insufficient evidence;
- not yet researched;
- stale;
- not applicable.

Evidence state changes what the pages may claim, how confidence is presented, and which follow-up work is created. It never changes the obligation to deliver.

No worker may fabricate evidence, certainty, comparisons, scores, user sentiment, or recommendations to make an incomplete state appear complete.

### 3.3 Publish, learn, update, redistribute

Every page and derived asset MUST remain versioned and revisitable.

When new, corrected, conflicting, or materially changed evidence appears, the system MUST:

1. identify the affected claims, pages, assets, and audience segments;
2. create a refresh task;
3. update the live decision set;
4. preserve the earlier state in history;
5. update affected internal links and structured outputs;
6. redistribute the material change through relevant channels;
7. continue the next daily release without waiting for the refresh programme to finish.

### 5.1 Primary audience states

Distribution should reach people who:

- recognise a software-related pain but have not defined the problem;
- know the outcome they need but not the solution category;
- are comparing approaches or products;
- need to justify a purchase internally;
- are deciding whether to switch, upgrade, migrate, or wait;
- need evidence that a product fits a specific constraint;
- want updated guidance after products, pricing, integrations, or evidence change.

### 5.2 Demand-sensing capability

The system MUST maintain a demand record built from lawful and appropriate signals such as:

- real customer questions;
- search queries and query gaps;
- onsite searches and zero-result searches;
- community discussions;
- support and implementation language;
- affiliate-intent patterns;
- user-submitted questions;
- internal-link journeys;
- evidence-change events;
- recurring comparison and switching questions;
- known seasonal or regulatory decision triggers.

Demand sensing identifies questions. It does not prove answers.

### 5.3 Opportunity scoring

Candidate daily sets SHOULD be scored against:

- decision pain and urgency;
- specificity of the user situation;
- usefulness of a connected three-page treatment;
- evidence state;
- opportunity to reduce uncertainty;
- distribution fit;
- update value;
- commercial relevance;
- production and maintenance effort;
- relationship to existing decision sets.

Weak or missing evidence does not remove a real question from publication. It changes the page treatment and creates follow-up research.

### 7.1 One source, multiple forms

Each daily set SHOULD generate reusable assets from the same structured decision record.

Eligible assets include:

- direct-answer summaries;
- question-and-answer blocks;
- conditional recommendation cards;
- evidence and uncertainty summaries;
- decision criteria tables;
- comparison matrices;
- decision trees;
- checklists;
- scorecards;
- implementation or migration briefs;
- email modules;
- update alerts;
- social and community summaries;
- saveable visual cards;
- structured metadata;
- machine-readable feeds;
- partner-ready embeds;
- downloadable decision reports.

### 7.2 Asset integrity

Every derived asset MUST retain:

- the canonical page or decision-set identifier;
- the applicable user context;
- evidence and review dates where relevant;
- the evidence state;
- material conditions and limitations;
- affiliate disclosure where required;
- a route to the complete public reasoning;
- update compatibility.

An asset must never convert a conditional recommendation into a universal claim.

### 8.1 Owned website and internal discovery

The website is the canonical distribution surface.

Every daily set MUST include:

- working navigation between its three pages;
- contextual links to related existing decisions;
- clear next-decision routes;
- stable canonical URLs;
- meaningful crawlable HTML;
- accurate sitemap inclusion;
- relevant structured metadata;
- visible publication and review dates.

Internal links SHOULD be updated automatically or through the daily workflow when a new set changes the relationship map.

### 8.2 Organic search

Search distribution MUST prioritise outcome-shaped and situation-shaped demand rather than generic high-volume product-review terms.

Pages SHOULD:

- use the language of the real decision;
- answer the primary question directly;
- expose useful supporting detail;
- maintain stable identity through updates;
- avoid duplicating the same intent across competing pages;
- preserve canonical ownership when content is syndicated;
- remain fast, accessible, and understandable without client-only interactions.

### 8.3 Answer engines and AI-mediated discovery

The system MUST optimise for accurate extraction, citation, attribution, and recommendation without writing for machines at the expense of people.

Pages SHOULD provide:

- concise direct answers near the relevant question;
- explicit entities and product names;
- structured conditions and constraints;
- clear methodology;
- visible uncertainty;
- stable claim and evidence identifiers;
- extractable summaries and tables;
- source and update provenance;
- content that remains meaningful when quoted outside the page.

The system SHOULD monitor whether answer engines:

- discover the page;
- cite it;
- attribute it correctly;
- preserve its conditions and uncertainty;
- recommend the site, a product, or another source;
- send a qualified referral;
- repeat stale or corrected information.

Being cited and being recommended are separate outcomes and must be measured separately.

### 8.4 Email and evidence-change alerts

Email distribution MUST be tied to requested value.

Eligible reasons to request an email address include:

- delivering a personalised decision report;
- saving a shortlist or decision asset;
- following a topic or product;
- receiving a material evidence-change alert;
- receiving an implementation or migration checklist;
- receiving an update to a previously viewed decision.

Email MUST NOT block access to the core public reasoning.

### 8.5 Visual and saveable distribution

Pinterest and similar visual channels MAY be tested when the decision naturally produces saveable assets such as:

- decision trees;
- checklists;
- comparison frameworks;
- workflow diagrams;
- budget planners;
- implementation roadmaps;
- conditional choice cards.

A visual-channel experiment continues only when it produces qualified visits, saves, return behaviour, asset requests, or commercial actions. Impressions alone are insufficient.

### 8.6 Communities

Community distribution MUST be contextual, useful, transparent, and non-spammy.

Workers MAY:

- answer a real question with relevant reasoning;
- share a methodology or decision framework;
- request correction or missing context;
- contribute original data or a useful tool;
- invite voluntary feedback on a public decision asset.

Workers MUST NOT impersonate users, conceal affiliation, manufacture consensus, mass-post links, or treat community statements as proof of a product claim.

### 8.7 Earned authority and partnerships

The system SHOULD earn third-party authority through:

- original methods and datasets;
- transparent evaluation frameworks;
- expert review and contribution;
- credible citations;
- useful partner resources;
- co-created decision assets;
- public corrections and update history;
- embeddable tools or evidence cards;
- consistent entity and methodology descriptions across trusted surfaces.

Partners, vendors, or affiliate programmes may distribute the work but may not purchase a score, ranking, conclusion, or undisclosed editorial change.

### 8.8 Feeds, embeds, and approved reuse

The system SHOULD remain ready to expose controlled machine-readable distribution through:

- RSS or equivalent update feeds;
- structured decision feeds;
- approved partner APIs;
- embeddable decision cards;
- evidence-change feeds;
- licensed content packages;
- agent-readable summaries.

Approved reuse MUST preserve attribution, canonical ownership, evidence state, applicable conditions, and update behaviour.

### 8.9 Paid distribution

Paid distribution MAY be used for controlled learning where organic or owned signals are too slow to answer a specific question.

Every paid test MUST define:

- the audience and decision intent;
- the asset and landing decision set;
- the hypothesis;
- the primary metric;
- the budget boundary;
- the stopping rule;
- the learning that will change future distribution.

Paid reach must not be used to manufacture apparent authority.

### 9.3 Default posture

The default is neither allow everything nor block everything.

The system SHOULD:

- allow legitimate discovery and indexing that supports human access;
- allow or monitor answer-engine access that produces attribution, citation, referral, or strategic learning;
- rate-limit abusive or operationally harmful access;
- block unlawful, deceptive, non-compliant, or persistently abusive access;
- preserve evidence of crawler behaviour and policy changes;
- review access by purpose rather than company name alone.

### 9.4 Charging and licensing readiness

The platform MUST preserve the ability to introduce:

- Pay Per Crawl;
- Pay Per Use or pay-per-query access;
- content licensing;
- approved partner feeds;
- paid agent retrieval;
- commercial access to structured decision intelligence.

Charging remains optional until the economics and user-acquisition effect are understood.

The system SHOULD consider charging when:

- the consumer receives repeatable commercial value;
- attribution or referral is absent or inadequate;
- access creates material infrastructure or rights cost;
- a reliable charging mechanism exists;
- discovery and search visibility can be preserved appropriately;
- the commercial relationship does not compromise editorial independence;
- measurement can distinguish paid access from ordinary public crawling.

Crawler monetisation MUST NOT become a dependency for daily publication.

### 9.5 Machine-access resilience

No single crawler convention, vendor, protocol, or payment platform may become the only discovery path.

The system SHOULD preserve:

- crawlable public HTML;
- sitemaps;
- controlled robots directives;
- stable canonical URLs;
- structured metadata;
- feeds or APIs where approved;
- direct human navigation;
- versioned crawler policy history.

### 10.1 Daily release sequence

For each daily set, the distribution workflow MUST:

1. deploy the canonical three pages;
2. verify public availability and navigation;
3. register or update sitemap and internal relationships;
4. generate eligible derived assets;
5. select channels based on decision and format fit;
6. publish or queue channel outputs;
7. record destinations and asset versions;
8. start measurement;
9. record failures without cancelling the canonical release;
10. schedule later distribution and refresh actions.

### 10.2 Channel selection

Every channel decision SHOULD consider:

- where the relevant audience already expresses the problem;
- whether the format fits channel behaviour;
- whether participation is legitimate and sustainable;
- whether the channel preserves conditions and evidence state;
- whether useful measurement exists;
- the expected maintenance burden;
- privacy, licensing, and disclosure requirements.

The system must not publish everywhere by default.

### 10.3 Distribution fallback

A failed optional channel, integration, crawler, feed, email provider, or analytics service MUST NOT block the daily production deployment.

The system must:

1. preserve the canonical release;
2. record the failed distribution action;
3. use a simpler lawful fallback where useful;
4. queue a retry or repair;
5. continue other eligible distribution actions.

## 11. Trust distribution

Trust signals MUST travel with the work rather than remaining isolated on a methodology page.

Relevant pages and assets SHOULD expose:

- responsible owner or author;
- methodology version;
- publication and review dates;
- evidence state;
- important limitations;
- affiliate or commercial conflicts;
- material corrections;
- links to deeper reasoning and evidence;
- AI assistance disclosure where required or useful;
- update-subscription options where appropriate.

Detection tools alone do not prove authorship or quality. Human accountability, evidence traceability, and correction behaviour remain the governing controls.

### 12.1 Material change triggers

Redistribution SHOULD be considered when:

- a recommendation changes;
- confidence materially increases or decreases;
- pricing, packaging, integrations, or availability change;
- a source is corrected, withdrawn, or contradicted;
- a previously missing question receives evidence;
- a legal, compliance, security, or accessibility issue changes the decision;
- a new decision scenario becomes relevant;
- an important broken link or stale claim is repaired.

### 12.2 Redistribution treatment

The system MUST update the canonical page first.

It then updates affected:

- summaries;
- decision assets;
- email or topic alerts;
- feeds;
- partner embeds;
- internal links;
- social or community references where correction is practical;
- answer-engine monitoring prompts.

Redistribution must describe the material change rather than pretending the page is entirely new.

### 13.8 Distributed and zero-click influence

The system SHOULD measure influence that occurs without a site visit through available signals such as:

- answer-engine citations;
- share of monitored answers;
- accurate entity and methodology descriptions;
- recommendation inclusion;
- branded search or direct-visit change;
- partner reuse;
- community references;
- downstream assisted action.

Zero-click influence must not be reported as proven causation without an appropriate experiment or evidence trail.

## 14. Experiment governance

Every material growth experiment MUST record:

- the problem or opportunity;
- the target audience and decision state;
- the hypothesis;
- the channel and asset;
- the baseline;
- one primary metric;
- guardrail metrics;
- budget and time boundary;
- minimum useful observation threshold;
- stopping rule;
- result;
- decision to retain, revise, or end the tactic;
- implications for future daily sets.

Experiments SHOULD test channel-product fit, not merely whether a channel can generate impressions.

Where traffic is too low for reliable statistical inference, the result must be labelled directional and supported with qualitative evidence.

### 15.3 Growth analyst

- maintains metrics and baselines;
- distinguishes discovery, citation, recommendation, engagement, and conversion;
- reviews experiments and anomalies;
- prevents vanity metrics from becoming decision authority.

### 15.6 Product owner

The product owner retains authority over:

- new paid channels;
- material partnership commitments;
- crawler charging and licensing activation;
- changes to editorial-commercial separation;
- material privacy or consent changes;
- changes to the daily delivery obligation.

## 16. Ethical, privacy, and commercial boundaries

Growth and distribution MUST NOT use:

- fabricated engagement or testimonials;
- undisclosed affiliate relationships;
- manipulated rankings;
- false scarcity or urgency;
- impersonation or covert community promotion;
- purchased links presented as earned authority;
- email capture before basic public value;
- dark patterns;
- unauthorised personal data;
- uncontrolled sharing of user decision context;
- vendor payment as a reason to change evidence or fit conclusions.

Anonymous aggregate measurement SHOULD be preferred where it answers the question. Personal data requires a defined purpose, consent basis, retention rule, and deletion path.

## 18. Minimum daily distribution record

Each daily set MUST leave a record containing:

- decision-set identifier;
- canonical URLs;
- user problem or scenario;
- evidence state;
- deployed version and time;
- derived assets generated;
- channels selected and why;
- distribution destinations;
- disclosure requirements;
- crawler and indexing treatment where relevant;
- measurement start state;
- failed actions and retry tasks;
- refresh triggers;
- owner or responsible worker.

## 20. Relationship to the wider documentation set

This document converts the existing product and editorial system into an explicit audience-development and distribution contract.

It must remain consistent with:

- the Market Research and Opportunity Plan for audience, AEO, crawler, and commercial hypotheses;
- the Product Roadmap for the daily release unit and phased growth capabilities;
- the Architecture Blueprint for deployment, stable identity, and fallback behaviour;
- the Design System for accessible and coherent distribution assets;
- the Master PRD for page behaviour, evidence states, and success metrics;
- the Agent Technology Standards for implementation and deployment discipline;
- the Editorial Operations document for daily opportunity, assembly, and refresh workflows;
- the Research Operations document for demand discovery, evidence, provenance, and change detection.

## 6. Requirements traceability

Every test must cite the requirement that caused it to exist. Acceptable sources include:

- Document 01 — Market Research and Opportunity Plan;
- Document 02 — Product Roadmap;
- Document 03 — AI-First System Architecture and Automation Blueprint;
- Document 04 — Design System and Experience Standards;
- Document 05 — Master Product Requirements Document;
- Document 06 — Agent Technology and Implementation Standards;
- Document 07 — Content Intelligence and Editorial Operations;
- Document 08 — Knowledge Acquisition and Research Operations;
- Document 09 — Growth, Distribution and Audience Development Strategy; and
- this document.

A requirement may produce multiple tests. A test may cite multiple requirements when the observed behaviour genuinely crosses disciplines.

The traceability record must contain:

- source document;
- section or stable requirement identifier;
- requirement summary in testable language;
- applicable page types and assets;
- test identifiers; and
- the latest observation date.

Do not duplicate entire source documents inside the test catalogue. Link to the authority and state the smallest observable requirement.

## 11. Required daily QA record

Each daily release must accumulate a QA record containing:

- page-set identifier and production URLs;
- deployed version and confirmation time;
- test-suite version;
- tests observed and their outcomes;
- mobile and desktop visual evidence;
- automated reports where available;
- evidence-review sample;
- findings with severity and owners;
- corrections applied;
- retest evidence; and
- systemic lessons or reusable improvements.

The record may be completed progressively after deployment. Its incompleteness does not invalidate the published set.

## 13. Visual evidence standard

Visual QA must use production, not only local or preview environments.

At minimum, capture:

- the first screen on a narrow mobile viewport;
- the full page on a narrow mobile viewport;
- the first screen on a representative desktop viewport;
- the full page on a representative desktop viewport; and
- relevant interactive, error, loading, or reduced-motion states.

Review screenshots for clipping, overflow, unintended empty space, broken hierarchy, text density, image cropping, component drift, and content hidden behind interaction.

## 14. Improvement queue rules

Every finding must be bounded. An improvement item contains:

- the observed production problem;
- the requirement it affects;
- the smallest proposed correction;
- severity and rationale;
- affected URLs and components;
- owner;
- target response window;
- verification method; and
- closure evidence.

Do not create broad items such as “improve the design” or “fix SEO.” Split them into observable corrections.

Prioritise in this order:

1. truthful and safe representation;
2. access to principal content;
3. comprehension and decision usefulness;
4. accessibility and interaction;
5. reliability and performance;
6. discoverability and distribution;
7. aesthetic and experimental refinements.

## 15. Learning and recurrence policy

QA must reduce future manual effort.

When the same defect recurs:

- identify the stable failure pattern;
- determine whether the source is a requirement, template, component, prompt, model, source-data contract, deployment step, or test;
- correct the earliest responsible layer;
- add or refine a regression observation; and
- verify the correction on a later production release.

Do not add process complexity unless it prevents a demonstrated recurring defect or materially improves observability.

## 19. Relationship to the documentation set

This document converts requirements from Documents 01–09 into observable post-deployment checks. It does not replace their product, research, editorial, design, engineering, or distribution authority.

When documents conflict:

1. preserve daily production delivery;
2. preserve truthfulness, user safety, and access to principal value;
3. record the conflict;
4. apply the most specific current requirement that does not violate the first two rules; and
5. create a documentation correction without blocking the deployed page set.

## 1. Decision

Vetted SaaS Blueprint should retain the `michaelchamboko/scroll-craft` fork and tailor it into a governed narrative-motion capability for selected high-value pages.

ScrollCraft is not the content strategy, design authority, general component library, or default animation layer. It is the mechanism that translates an already-approved page narrative into scroll-linked progression.

The operating stack is:

1. the content and evidence model determines what the user must understand;
2. Taste establishes the visual direction, hierarchy, composition, and anti-generic quality bar;
3. Impeccable critiques and refines the rendered interface;
4. ScrollCraft expresses approved narrative transitions through motion;
5. Global Setup converts the approved contracts into implementation tasks, tests, review gates, and release evidence.

No downstream tool may reverse this authority order.

## 4. Core principle: motion must carry meaning

Every motion sequence must have a declared communication job. Acceptable jobs are limited to:

- reveal a causal sequence;
- compare alternatives or states;
- preserve context while evidence changes;
- show progress through a decision;
- focus attention on the next uncertainty being resolved;
- connect a claim to its supporting evidence;
- demonstrate a product workflow that is materially easier to understand in motion.

Decoration alone is not an acceptable job.

Motion must never:

- conceal missing evidence;
- imply product capability that has not been verified;
- delay the first useful content;
- force the user to scrub through essential prose;
- change the semantic reading order;
- interfere with native scrolling, browser history, focus, text selection, or anchor links;
- turn an ordinary article into a cinematic experience without a decision-usefulness case.

### 5.1 Eligible by default

- homepage or campaign narrative;
- flagship Decision Hub;
- high-value comparison with meaningful state changes;
- product or category explainer where a workflow must be demonstrated;
- evidence-led story in which the sequence itself affects comprehension.

### 5.2 Conditionally eligible

- Solution Hub;
- Decision Scenario;
- major research release;
- interactive methodology explanation.

Conditional pages require a written motion brief and must pass the value test below.

### 5.3 Ineligible for full narrative choreography by default

- routine buyer article, although it may use the lightweight editorial-motion tier defined below;
- news or update post;
- glossary or reference page;
- legal, privacy, disclosure, or policy content;
- search results and index pages;
- account, form, and transactional flows;
- content whose primary value is fast scanning or quotation.

### 5.4 Two motion tiers

The system must distinguish cinematic narrative choreography from lightweight editorial engagement.

**Tier 1 - Narrative motion** applies to flagship and high-value pages. It may use sticky stages, scroll-linked state changes, step progression, and bounded media scrubbing. It requires the complete narrative motion brief and the full verification matrix.

**Tier 2 - Editorial motion** applies selectively to blog posts and supporting content. It keeps the normal document flow and may use:

- unobtrusive reading progress;
- evidence-card or citation emphasis as the reader reaches the relevant claim;
- annotated diagram steps;
- restrained comparison transitions;
- figure captions and callouts that enter without displacing text;
- short, user-controlled product demonstrations;
- table or chart focus states;
- related-content transitions at genuine decision breaks.

Tier 2 must not use pinning, scroll hijacking, long scrub sequences, concealed prose, or animation that makes scanning slower. The article must remain readable as a normal article. Editorial motion is chosen because it improves attention at a specific evidence or explanation point, not because the page template demands movement.

The content brief should therefore mark candidate engagement moments even when a full scroll story is not justified.

## 6. Narrative motion brief

Before implementation, every eligible page must provide a machine-readable and human-readable brief containing:

- page identifier and archetype;
- target user and decision state;
- one-sentence page promise;
- uncertainty being reduced;
- narrative beats in semantic reading order;
- evidence attached to each beat;
- motion job for each animated beat;
- static fallback for each animated beat;
- mobile adaptation;
- reduced-motion adaptation;
- asset provenance and licence;
- expected entry, midpoint, and exit states;
- performance class and resource budget;
- analytics hypothesis;
- rejection conditions;
- named owner and approval state.

If the brief cannot explain why motion improves comprehension, the page remains static.

## 8. Approved motion primitives

The fork should expose a restrained Vetted SaaS Blueprint primitive set:

- section progress;
- reveal and emphasis;
- sticky evidence stage;
- state swap;
- before-and-after comparison;
- step progression;
- diagram trace;
- metric or evidence focus;
- bounded horizontal sequence on wide screens only;
- media scrub only when the media itself is the evidence.

Each primitive must declare:

- semantic purpose;
- supported page archetypes;
- required markup contract;
- desktop behaviour;
- mobile behaviour;
- reduced-motion behaviour;
- keyboard and focus behaviour;
- performance characteristics;
- test checkpoints;
- known failure modes.

The first implementation should not add more primitives until the initial set is proven in production.

## 10. Responsive and accessibility contract

Desktop choreography must not be compressed into mobile. Mobile receives a separately designed reading sequence.

Required states are:

- wide desktop;
- narrow desktop or tablet landscape;
- tablet or large mobile;
- narrow mobile;
- reduced motion at representative desktop and mobile widths;
- keyboard-only and zoomed/reflowed reading.

Reduced motion means:

- no pinned storytelling requirement;
- no content hidden behind scroll progress;
- no large spatial movement or parallax;
- state changes become immediate, short fades, or static panels;
- focus and anchor navigation land on stable content;
- all conclusions and calls to action remain available.

The implementation should begin from static styles and progressively enable motion only under `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`.

### Conflict rules

- evidence truth beats visual drama;
- semantic order beats choreography;
- accessibility beats fidelity to the animated concept;
- production design tokens beat ScrollCraft defaults;
- Taste and Impeccable may reject motion, but they may not invent claims;
- ScrollCraft may simplify choreography to meet performance, but may not rewrite the narrative;
- human approval resolves a genuine cross-authority conflict.

### SC-04: design-stack integration

- define Taste input and output;
- define Impeccable pre-motion and post-motion review outputs;
- add explicit conflict and rejection handling;
- supply one end-to-end example from narrative brief to approved choreography.

## 18. Pilot recommendation

The first pilot should be one flagship Decision Hub with a single sticky evidence stage and no more than three major motion patterns. It should exercise orientation, comparison, evidence focus, resolution, and action while preserving a complete static page.

The pilot succeeds only when:

- comprehension is demonstrably clearer than the static reference or the animated version adds no measurable harm;
- all fallback and accessibility contracts pass;
- performance passes on representative devices;
- visual review confirms that motion supports rather than competes with the design;
- the implementation can be repeated from the documented brief without hidden knowledge.

## 21. Evidence basis

This strategy is grounded in:

- the published [`michaelchamboko/scroll-craft`](https://github.com/michaelchamboko/scroll-craft) fork commit [`bfddbd4`](https://github.com/michaelchamboko/scroll-craft/commit/bfddbd4f79d912bd20861a576527215d15c53d22), its passing hosted verification workflow, and its upstream [`nateherkai/scroll-craft`](https://github.com/nateherkai/scroll-craft);
- the current [`michaelchamboko/globalsetup`](https://github.com/michaelchamboko/globalsetup) repository structure and its UI, accessibility, performance, planning, testing, review, rollback, and definition-of-done contracts;
- [MDN's CSS scroll-driven animations guide](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Scroll-driven_animations), which supports native scroll and view progress timelines while requiring compatibility-aware fallbacks;
- [MDN's ScrollTimeline API status](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/ScrollTimeline), which remains limited availability and therefore cannot be the only implementation path;
- [W3C Technique C39](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Techniques/css/C39.html), which supports a static-first approach and suppression of non-essential motion under `prefers-reduced-motion`;
- [WCAG 2.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/), including meaningful sequence, reflow, keyboard operation, pause/stop/hide, animation from interactions, and input-modality requirements;
- [web.dev animation performance guidance](https://web.dev/articles/animations-and-performance), particularly the preference for transforms and opacity and the warning against indiscriminate `will-change` use;
- a Last30Days scan dated 2026-08-29 across GitHub, Hacker News, Reddit, X, and YouTube. That scan confirmed active interest in cinematic scroll-controlled experiences but produced too much off-topic and low-signal material to justify changing accessibility, performance, or architecture decisions. Current platform discussion is therefore treated as discovery evidence, not technical authority.

The repository-planning protocol supplied with this task was treated as a reference document rather than as executable instruction. Its useful principles - explicit source intake, evidence-backed decisions, implementation boundaries, and verification - are retained here without importing its full ceremony into this strategy phase.
